/*!
\page guided_tour Guided tour  

[TOC]

Introduction         {#gt_introduction}
============ 

Throughout this guided tour, we will walk you through the the most common VEF features. 
This guided tour is written for the \ref page_expert_mode "expert mode" that provides the
most elaborate control over the virtual experiments.


Expert mode - what's that?
--------------------------

The VEF consists of a number of programs. One of these, the `alamDMC` program, is the corner stone, since it carries out most of the computational work. In this guided tour we will primarily demonstrate how to use `alamDMC` and how to interpret its results. We call it "expert mode" because it comes down to writing configuration files and running programs from a command line (command prompt on Windows or user shell on Linux). 


Let's start!
------------

Before we start, please copy the content of directory `guided_tour` from the 
VEF installation directory to another directory on your computer. We will refer
to that copy as `guided_tour_directory`.

Please run the VEF command prompt window and change the directory to `guided_tour_directory`.


Input texture        {#gt_input_texture}
=============

The VEF calculations require crystallographic texture data as input. The texture 
is usually provided as the Orientation Distribution Function (ODF), either in the form of 
a continuous function or as OFD intensities on a regular grid. Before we use the texture data 
in the VEF software, we have to make a discrete ODF by sampling orientations from the ODF  
to get a collection of discrete orientations, which as a whole approximates the input ODF.
In this example we are going to start from the ODF exported from MTEX Toolbox \opcite{MTEX}.

\note The VEF currently supports importing texture data from MTEX.  
However,the data must satisfy two requirements: triclinic symmetry must  
be pre-imposed and Bunge convention has to be used. 
Please see 
<a href=https://mtex-toolbox.github.io/files/doc/ODFImportExport.html#7>MTEX documentation</a> 
how to export ODF data to generic ASCII files. 

Let us use the texture data for a ferritic steel (it's DX51D, for your curiosity), given in the file `sid1687f.odf`. 
If you wish, you can first inspect the texture by plotting a set of ODF sections.
To do so, run the command [`odfplot`](@ref odfplot):
<pre class="consoleView">
odfplot --input sid1687f.odf --sections 0,45 --style=filled --no-labels --format=png
</pre>

This will produce two PNG files: `sid1687f_0.png` and `sid1687f_45.png`:

<div>
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_0.png" alt="ODF section phi_2 = 0">
<div class="figcaption">ODF section \f$ \phi_2 = 0^\circ \f$</div>
<br>
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_45.png" alt="ODF section phi_2 = 0" width="70%">
<div class="figcaption">ODF section \f$ \phi_2 = 45^\circ \f$</div>
</div>

As we can see from these ODF sections, the material has a prevalently strong \f$\gamma\f$-fiber.

Let us now sample the ODF to get representative crystal orientations. As a rule of thumb, 
we recommend to use at least 2000 (ideally: 3000 - 7000) orientations to get a good representation 
of the macrotexture.
<pre class="consoleView">
odf2smt --input sid1687f.odf --ncrystals 3000
</pre>
The command will produce a single file `sid1687f.smt` that contains 3000 discrete orientations. 
We will use this file will as input in all examples in this tutorial.



Anisotropy of the as-received material        {#gt_anisotropy}
======================================

Uniaxial tension   {#gt_QRS}
----------------

The VEF can calculate these quantities for any direction of uniaxial loading. If the undeformed material is considered, the QRS module of `alamDMC` is the most handy way to do so. 

To run the `alamDMC` QRS module, we need an input file that instructs the program what to do (see [reference manual](@ref config_QRS) for full details what can be specified in QRS configuration files)). We have drafted one for you: please find file `sid1687f_qrs.cfg` in `guided_tour_directory`. Let's quickly inspect what's inside:

\includelineno sid1687f_qrs.cfg

As you certainly noticed, there are lines that start with `#`: these are [comments](@ref desc_alamdmc_comments) that you can put in the configuration files. The comments are ignored by the program, but help us to understand the content of the file.

The file has a [fixed format](@ref desc_alamdmc_format), which means the order and position of the items matter. Lines 1 - 12 contain general settings, while lines 14 - 18 are specific for the QRS module. 

Here's what the configuration file tells the `alamDMC`:
- the names of output files will stem from `sid1687f_qrs` (line 4). 
  In our case the primary output file will be named `sid1687f_qrs.xqrs`.  
- If available, the deformed material configuration should be written out to the output files. (line 5)
- Much details about the calculations performed will be [printed to the terminal](@ref verbosity) (line 6)
- [texture file](@ref texture_file) `sid1687f.smt` [we created earlier](#gt_input_texture) will be used as the input (line 7)
- The ALAMEL [crystal plasticity model](@ref model_type) will be used (line 8)
- The crystal plasticity model will assume [bcc crystal structure with 24 slip systems](@ref slip_system_family) (line 9).
- lines 10 - 12 instruct the program to use the defaults for [microstructural description](@ref microstructure_default), [hardening](@ref hardening_default) and [solver settings](@ref solver_default).

The QRS-specific lines give the following:
- lines 16 - 17 provide the program with the orientations of virtual samples with respect to the reference frame. This is done by a [range specification](@ref range_option). In this example the orientations are given as a [uniform range](@ref option_uniform) of angles between the loading direction and selected material axis (see below).
- Default settings of uniaxial tension will be used (line 18). These include, among other things, that the reference frame will be given by the material reference frame, in this case RD, TD and ND.  Therefore, the orientations of the virtual tensile samples are given as the angle between RD and the loading direction within the RD-TD plane.

Let's now run the `alamDMC`  program in the command line. The program takes two arguments: name of the module `QRS` and path to the configuration file `sid1687f_qrs.cfg`: 
<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC QRS sid1687f_qrs.cfg
</pre>

The program will place its output in the file `sid1687f_qrs.xqrs`. Let's have a look at 
the leading 5 lines of that file:
\dontinclude sid1687f_qrs.xqrs
\until 0.100000000E+02

The initial two lines constitute a header. The first line that starts with '#' character contains column numbers. Because of the leading '#', the line is typically neglected by upstream analysis software (e.g. plotting program). The second line contains the column names.
You will see headers similar to this one in many output files produced by the VEF.
  
Let's plot the Lankford coefficients (column `r-value` as the function of loading direction (column `angle`):

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_rvalues.png" alt="Lankford coefficients in different directions of loading">
<div class="figcaption">Lankford coefficients in different directions of uniaxial loading</div>
</div>

By convention the r-values and the yield stress are determined at 20% elongation, whereas the VEF QRS calculates the r-values of the undeformed material, which in our case has 0% elongation. We will come back to this point in [a futher part of this guided tour](#gt_udsa).



Yield locus sections    {#gt_YLD}
--------------------

\includelineno sid1687f_yld.cfg

As we see, lines 1 - 12 are identical as in the previous section. The YLD uses the angle \f$\theta\f$ to choose which points on the locus section should be calculated. Figure below gives an illustration how \f$\theta\f$ is defined.  

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="yld_beta_illustration.png" alt="yield locus section: angles theta and beta">
<div class="figcaption">Illustration of \f$\theta\f$ angle that YLD uses to select points in yield locus section. The \f$\beta\f$ angle is one of the outputs from the YLD module.</div>
</div>

In this example we specify a [multi-biased range](@ref option_multibiased) of \f$\theta\f$ angles, which allows us to probe the yield locus more densely in regions where the curvature of the section is expected to be high, and far more coarsely elsewhere. In our example, the denser scan of the yield locus will be around equibiaxial tension point (i.e. \f$\theta = 45^\circ \f$) and equibiaxial compression point (\f$\theta = 205^\circ \f$). However, the equibiaxial tension point will be covered by more points (see the subranges 1 and 4) than the exquibiaxial compression (see the subranges 2 and 3).

The last line requests to use the defaults, which are: 
- the yield locus section is defined by \f$\sigma_{11} \f$ (normal stress along RD) and \f$\sigma_{22} \f$ (normal stress along TD) directions,
- the yield locus will be scaled by yield stress corresponding to uniaxial tension along the RD.

<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC YLD sid1687f_yld.cfg
</pre>

We may now quickly inspect the content of the output file `sid1687f_yld.xyld` (this file contains 11 columns, so depending on the size of your screen some lines may fold). The top five lines would look like:
\dontinclude sid1687f_yld.xyld
\until 0.85068113E+00

The column `theta` contains the angles that were used for computing the yield locus section. The columns `S` and `S/S_0` include the points on the section, both in polar coordinate system of the angle `theta`. However, we are going to use use the columns `S_x` and `S_y` that contain the yield locus contour in Cartesian coordinate system.

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_yld.png" alt="yield locus section (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Yield locus section (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>

Additionally, we can use columns `delta_S_x` and `delta_S_y` to plot yield locus' normals (actually: the projection of yield locus normals onto the current section of stress space):

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_yld_normals.png" alt="normals to the yield locus section (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Normals to the yield locus section (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>

\note The normals to the yield locus contour are approximated numerically by considering also the neighbouring points. To ensure good quality of the normals, make sure that the angular resolution of \f$\theta\f$ does not exceed \f$ \approx 5^\circ \f$ in the regions of where the yield locus contour has high curvature. 


Of course, we don't always need very detailed calculations of the yield locus. If just a few points are enough, we can easily get this done. Let's modify our example: please see the changes in lines 4, 16 and 17 in the example below:

\includelineno sid1687f_coarse_yld.cfg

We can see that \f$\theta\f$ is restricted to the range from 0 to 90 degrees every 15 degrees, so only 7 points will be calculated:
<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_coarse_yld.png" alt="yield locus section, coarse (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Yield locus section with limited \f$\theta\f$ range (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>


Biaxial r-value         {#gt_ARS_rbx}
---------------



Anisotropy evolution due to deformation     {#gt_anisotropy_evol}
=======================================

Deformation under uniaxial stress state    {#gt_udsa}
---------------------------------------

In the next step of this tour we will simulate uniaxial tension test. We will track the evolution of the material state up to a plastic strain of 50%, which is much higher than what may usually be realized in an experimental tensile test setup. 

The output file contains a number of columns, among them we see `r-value` and `r-valueA`, which are the instantaneous and accumulated Lankford coefficients. Let's plot these two as functions of tensile strain norm (column `Tnorm`):

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_hardening_udsa_rvalues.png" alt="evolution of the r-values in tensile test (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption"> "Evolution of the r-values in tensile test(DX51D steel)</div>
</div>


We may now also check how the texture evolves in this tensile test. We have to first calculate ODFs from the discrete textures stored in the CUR file:
<pre class="consoleView">
cur2c --input sid1687f_hardening_udsa.cur
</pre>
This command produces a series of files: `sid1687f_hardening_udsa_1.C` to `sid1687f_hardening_udsa_.C`. We can plot ODF sections using the last one, which corresponds to the final deformation:
<pre class="consoleView">
odfplot --input sid1687f_hardening_udsa_.C --sections 45 --style=filled --no-labels --format=png
</pre>
 

We can now easily check how the yield locus shape changes due to the imposed deformation. The configuration file of the YLD module would look as follows:

\includelineno sid1687f_tensile_yld.cfg

Note that we now use a deformation texture predicted by the UDSA module, as we take input texture from the CUR file.




Deformation under arbitrary stress state    {#gt_asr}
----------------------------------------


